+{
+ "cells": [
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "`Perc` replaces each observation with percentile values computed within its group. The output has one row per requested percentile level, with the level recorded in a `percentile` column. The default uses five levels, giving the endpoints and quartiles."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": null,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "import seaborn.objects as so\n",
+    "from seaborn import load_dataset\n",
+    "penguins = load_dataset('penguins')\n",
+    "\n",
+    "so.Plot(penguins, x='species', y='body_mass_g').add(so.Dot(), so.Perc())"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "Pass an integer to request that many evenly spaced levels from 0 to 100, or provide a list of explicit levels. The default percentile method is `linear`; NumPy also supports `inverted_cdf`, `averaged_inverted_cdf`, `closest_observation`, `interpolated_inverted_cdf`, `hazen`, `weibull`, `median_unbiased`, `normal_unbiased`, `lower`, `higher`, `midpoint`, and `nearest`."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": null,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "# Three evenly spaced levels\n",
+    "so.Plot(penguins, x='species', y='body_mass_g').add(so.Dot(), so.Perc(3))\n",
+    "\n",
+    "# Explicit percentile levels\n",
+    "so.Plot(penguins, x='species', y='body_mass_g').add(so.Dot(), so.Perc([10, 50, 90]))"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "Combine `Perc` with a range mark to show an interval between selected percentiles:"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": null,
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "so.Plot(penguins, x='species', y='body_mass_g').add(\n",
+    "    so.Range(), so.Perc([10, 90])\n",
+    ")"
+   ]
+  }
+ ],
